WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
Rosa bought a deferred annuity in her own name with money

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.540
on which she already paid income tax.

3
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.280
It is outside an individual retirement account or employer retirement plan.

4
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.020
This is a nonqualified annuity for our examples.

5
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.320
Its growth generally can accumulate without annual income tax while held

6
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.680
by an individual, but access to the money can create taxable

7
00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:25.800
income. Before calculating anything, identify the owner, the contract's

8
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.300
issue date and whether the payment is a withdrawal, full surrender

9
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.620
or annuity income. Different rules apply to different events.

10
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.700
A personal annuity does not become an individual retirement account merely

11
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.260
because Rosa intends to use it during retirement.

12
00:00:40.794 --> 00:00:45.007
Rosa's teaching contract was issued in twenty twenty, has ninety thousand

13
00:00:45.007 --> 00:00:49.574
dollars of cash value and sixty thousand dollars of adjusted investment

14
00:00:49.574 --> 00:00:54.774
in the contract. There are no prior distributions or special adjustments.

15
00:00:55.514 --> 00:00:58.634
The difference is thirty thousand dollars of gain.

16
00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:03.814
Before the annuity starting date, an ordinary partial withdrawal from this

17
00:01:03.814 --> 00:01:08.554
contract generally takes taxable earnings before recovering investment.

18
00:01:08.554 --> 00:01:13.774
A twenty thousand dollar withdrawal is therefore entirely taxable ordinary

19
00:01:13.774 --> 00:01:17.894
income because thirty thousand dollars of gain is available.

20
00:01:18.794 --> 00:01:22.294
Do not divide the withdrawal in proportion to premiums and growth.

21
00:01:23.094 --> 00:01:28.074
Also do not copy the ordinary basis-first withdrawal treatment from a

22
00:01:28.074 --> 00:01:30.074
non-modified life insurance policy.

23
00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:34.536
Now return to the original ninety thousand dollar value and sixty

24
00:01:34.536 --> 00:01:38.136
thousand dollar investment, without making the earlier withdrawal.

25
00:01:38.956 --> 00:01:42.396
This time Rosa withdraws forty thousand dollars.

26
00:01:43.116 --> 00:01:47.396
The first thirty thousand dollars uses the available gain and is

27
00:01:47.396 --> 00:01:52.316
taxable. The remaining ten thousand dollars recovers investment without being

28
00:01:52.316 --> 00:01:56.976
taxed again. Her remaining investment is fifty thousand dollars under these

29
00:01:56.976 --> 00:02:01.576
simplified facts. The withdrawal is not forty thousand dollars of taxable

30
00:02:01.576 --> 00:02:06.196
gain, because the contract contained only thirty thousand dollars of gain.

31
00:02:06.636 --> 00:02:09.836
Nor is the entire payment a tax-free return of premiums.

32
00:02:10.716 --> 00:02:14.776
Establish the value, adjusted investment and actual transaction amount before

33
00:02:14.776 --> 00:02:16.156
choosing the taxable portion.

34
00:02:16.782 --> 00:02:20.882
A full surrender ends the contract and returns its remaining surrender

35
00:02:20.882 --> 00:02:25.322
proceeds. Suppose the insurer states that Rosa would receive eighty-five

36
00:02:25.322 --> 00:02:30.682
thousand dollars after all applicable surrender charges, and her unrecovered

37
00:02:30.682 --> 00:02:32.682
investment is sixty thousand dollars.

38
00:02:33.422 --> 00:02:35.382
Assume no debt or other tax adjustment.

39
00:02:36.162 --> 00:02:38.922
The resulting taxable gain is twenty-five thousand dollars.

40
00:02:39.862 --> 00:02:43.382
That calculation uses the stated net proceeds for this full surrender.

41
00:02:43.382 --> 00:02:47.842
It should not be confused with the partial-withdrawal rule, where gain

42
00:02:47.842 --> 00:02:51.442
generally uses cash value before surrender charges.

43
00:02:52.102 --> 00:02:54.962
Read whether the question describes a partial payment or complete

44
00:02:54.962 --> 00:03:00.122
termination. The same displayed account value does not make those

45
00:03:00.122 --> 00:03:05.616
two transactions identical. Change the owner to a forty-five-year-old with

46
00:03:05.616 --> 00:03:09.496
a twenty thousand dollar taxable withdrawal and no applicable exception.

47
00:03:10.316 --> 00:03:14.916
The additional federal tax on an early nonqualified annuity distribution is

48
00:03:14.916 --> 00:03:17.236
generally ten percent of the taxable portion.

49
00:03:17.956 --> 00:03:20.976
In this example that is two thousand dollars, in addition to

50
00:03:20.976 --> 00:03:25.996
ordinary income tax. An insurer's free-withdrawal allowance concerns its

51
00:03:25.996 --> 00:03:30.456
contract charge and does not automatically remove either federal tax.

52
00:03:31.256 --> 00:03:35.256
Exceptions to the additional tax include distributions after age fifty-nine

53
00:03:35.256 --> 00:03:40.836
and one half, qualifying disability, death and certain substantially

54
00:03:40.836 --> 00:03:45.276
equal periodic payments. Use the exception that actually applies.

55
00:03:46.076 --> 00:03:49.896
An education or first-home exception associated with an individual retirement

56
00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:53.696
account is not a blanket commercial-annuity exception.

57
00:03:54.546 --> 00:03:59.606
For a nonqualified annuity, taking a loan or pledging value

58
00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:03.806
generally can be treated as receiving a distribution under the applicable

59
00:04:03.806 --> 00:04:08.546
tax rule. Do not assume that the familiar loan treatment of

60
00:04:08.546 --> 00:04:13.106
an ordinary life policy applies to every insurance contract.

61
00:04:13.666 --> 00:04:18.366
The amount treated as distributed must still be evaluated using the

62
00:04:18.366 --> 00:04:20.746
correct gain and basis rules.

63
00:04:21.646 --> 00:04:26.326
Rosa should obtain the insurer's transaction details before deciding what to

64
00:04:26.326 --> 00:04:31.046
do. On the exam, follow a consistent sequence:

65
00:04:31.966 --> 00:04:37.326
identify the nonqualified contract, distinguish withdrawal from income

66
00:04:37.566 --> 00:04:42.926
or full surrender, calculate the taxable amount, and then consider

67
00:04:42.926 --> 00:04:48.106
any additional tax. Keeping those steps separate prevents several

68
00:04:48.106 --> 00:04:49.346
common answer traps.


